/target
**/*.rs.bk
Cargo.lock.bak

# local environment
.env

# ...but the scaffold ships a .env for the project `arc new` generates,
# and the catalog reads it with include_str!. Ignored, it is absent from a
# fresh clone and the crate does not compile -- which is exactly how CI
# broke. These two are template payload, not anyone's local secrets.
!src/templates/files/**/.env
!src/templates/files/**/.env.example

# editor
.vscode/
.idea/

# node (frontend assets in generated apps)
node_modules/
dist/

# the built guide -- `mdbook build docs` writes it, CI rebuilds it
/docs/book/

# SQLite test databases. `just db-test` and the CI database job point
# ARCATURE_TEST_DB_URL at a file in the working directory; the driver also
# writes `-wal` and `-shm` alongside it.
/arcature_test_*.db
/arcature_test_*.db-wal
/arcature_test_*.db-shm

# `just geiger` keeps its fresh report here when the counts move, so the
# evidence survives a failed run without a second cold build. The accepted
# answer is `unsafe-baseline.<host-target>.txt`, which is committed.
/unsafe-report.txt

# `just coverage` leaves its summary here for the same reason, and reads the
# percentage back out of it -- so a run that went red can be re-read, and
# `just coverage-accept` can record the number without instrumenting the
# suite a second time. The accepted answer is
# `coverage-baseline.<host-target>.txt`, which is committed.
/coverage-report.txt

# The load suite appends its measurements here when `ARCATURE_LOAD` is set.
# Unlike the two above there is no gate reading it back: throughput and
# latency describe the runner as much as the code, so the accepted answer
# `load-baseline.<host-target>.txt` is a record for a human and not a
# threshold for a check. See `tests/load_profile.rs` for why.
/load-report.txt

# Agent worktrees. `git worktree add .claude/worktrees/<name>` puts a whole
# checkout in here, and an unignored one turns `git add -A` into a commit
# containing a gitlink to a branch that exists on nobody else's machine.
/.claude/
